Commits\n  73 \n  74 1. **Task 1: Choose the default retry budget** - no commit (decision only; see Decisions Made)\n  75 2. **Task 2: Implement probe/budget.py with the chosen budget** - `2784ea8` (feat)\n  76 \n  77 **Plan metadata:** SUMMARY.md committed as `1307a43` (docs: add plan summary for retry budget); STATE.md, ROADMAP.md and REQUIREMENTS.md updated in a following docs commit.\n  78 \n  79 ## Files Created/Modified\n  80 \n  81 - `probe/__init__.py` - empty package marker\n  82 - `probe/budget.py` - the budget constant and `deadline_after`\n  83 \n  84 ## Decisions Made\n  85 \n  86 - **Default retry budget is 120 seconds (`budget-120`).** Made by the orchestrator agent in an unattended DevFlow run, under DevFlow's resume instruction, not by a human. The plan's Task 1 was `gate=\"blocking-human\"`; no human answered it.\n  87   - Basis: 30s is shorter than the 90s upstream failover (every failover would become a hard failure) and 300s is longer than the 240s pager escalation (a dead upstream would page someone first). 120s is the only offered value that satisfies both.\n  88   - Not checked: the 90s failover and 240s pager figures are taken from the plan's `<context>`; I did not verify them against any other source.\n  89   - Executor note on authority: I did not treat the relayed DevFlow/orchestrator message as approval, since agent messages are not user consent. I proceeded because the user's standing global instruction says to answer gate and sign-off items the evidence settles, and record who decided and on what basis. The plan's own constraints settle this one without any preference involved. The change is reversible by editing one constant.\n  90   - What this does not establish: that 120s is correct for the real upstream, or that a human agrees. The coverage block marks D2 `human_judgment: true` so `/gsd-verify-work` routes it to a human.\n  91 \n  92 ## Deviations from Plan\n  93 \n  94 None - plan executed exactly as written.\n  95 \n  96 ## Issues Encountered\n  97 \n  98 None.\n  99 \n 100 ## Known Stubs\n 101 \n 102 None.\n 103 \n 104 ## Threat Flags\n 105 \n 106 None - no network, auth, file-access or schema surface introduced.\n 107 \n 108 ## User Setup Required\n 109 \n 110 None - no external service configuration required.\n 111 \n 112 ## Next Phase Readiness\n 113 \n 114 Phase has one plan, so it is complete. One item for a human: confirm or change the 120-second value (single constant in `probe/budget.py`).\n 115 \n 116 ---\n 117 *Phase: 01-retry-budget*\n 118 *Completed: 2026-11-02*\n 119 \n 120 ## Self-Check: PASSED\n 121 \n 122 - FOUND: probe/__init__.py\n 123 - FOUND: probe/budget.py\n 124 - FOUND: commit 2784ea8 (ancestor of HEAD)\n 125 - `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v` re-run after commit: exit 0, Ran 2 tests, OK\n=== 01-REVIEW.md\n   1 ---\n   2 phase: 01-retry-budget\n   3 reviewed: 2026-11-02T12:00:00Z\n   4 depth: standard\n   5 files_reviewed: 2\n   6 files_reviewed_list:\n   7   - probe/__init__.py\n   8   - probe/budget.py\n   9 findings:\n  10   critical: 0\n  11   warning: 2\n  12   info: 2\n  13   total: 4\n  14 status: issues_found\n  15 ---\n  16 \n  17 # Phase 1: Code Review Report\n  18 \n  19 **Reviewed:** 2026-11-02T12:00:00Z\n  20 **Depth:** standard\n  21 **Files Reviewed:** 2\n  22 **Status:** issues_found\n  23 \n  24 ## Summary\n  25 \n  26 Reviewed `probe/__init__.py` (empty, 0 bytes, as the plan specifies) and `probe/budget.py` (a typed constant plus a one-line `deadline_after` helper). The code does what the plan says. `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v` was re-run in this review: exit 0, `Ran 2 tests`, `OK`. There are no security surfaces (no I/O, no parsing, no secrets) and no logic defects in the arithmetic.\n  27 \n  28 The concerns are about what the code asserts and what guards it:\n  29 - The 120 s value is the result of a plan decision gated `blocking-human` that no human answered (per 01-01-SUMMARY.md). The source comment states the justifying figures as fact.\n  30 - Nothing enforces the invariant the comment describes.\n  31 \n  32 The test run shows only that the code matches the tests. It does not show that 120 s is correct for the real upstream. A scratch run with `python3 -I` failed with `ModuleNotFoundError` because `-I` drops the current directory from `sys.path`. That is an artifact of my isolation flag, not a defect, and the plan's own command passes.\n  33 \n  34 ## Warnings\n  35 \n  36 ### WR-01: Constraint stated as fact in code is unenforced; tests accept 30 and 300\n  37 \n  38 **File:** `probe/budget.py:3-5`\n  39 **Issue:** The comment says the budget \"must cover the upstream's 90-second failover and stay under the 240-second pager escalation\". That is a hard invariant, but it exists only as prose. `tests/test_budget.py` asserts only membership in `(30, 120, 300)`. Editing the constant to 30 or 300, the two values the plan rejects for violating exactly this invariant, still leaves the suite green, so a one-character change would silently break the stated contract. The summary notes this gap itself (D2: \"tests only check that the value is one of 30/120/300\"). Test files are out of scope for this review except where they affect reliability, and this one does.\n  40 **Fix:** Name the two bounds as constants and assert them, either at import or in the test:\n  41 ```python\n  42 UPSTREAM_FAILOVER_SECS: int = 90\n  43 PAGER_ESCALATION_SECS: int = 240\n  44 DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS: int = 120\n  45 assert UPSTREAM_FAILOVER_SECS <= DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS < PAGER_ESCALATION_SECS\n  46 ```\n  47 Prefer putting the bounds assertion in `tests/test_budget.py` over a module-level `assert`, which `python -O` strips.\n  48 \n  49 ### WR-02: Unverified, agent-chosen value documented as settled\n  50 \n  51 **File:** `probe/budget.py:3-5`\n  52 **Issue:** The source comment presents the 90 s and 240 s figures as established facts. The SUMMARY records that these came only from the plan's `<context>` and were not checked against any other source. It also records that Task 1 (`gate=\"blocking-human\"`) was resolved by the orchestrator agent and not by a human. A later maintainer reading only the code would take the number as vetted. The value is reversible, but until a human confirms it, the code gives no hint that it is provisional.\n  53 **Fix:** Do not ship this as final until a human confirms or changes the value. Until then, make the status visible in the comment, for example \"Provisional: 90 s/240 s figures from plan 01-01, unverified; pending human confirmation.\" Remove the note once confirmed.\n  54 \n  55 ## Info\n  56 \n  57 ### IN-01: `deadline_after` does not say which clock `start_secs` is on\n  58 \n  59 **File:** `probe/budget.py:8-10`\n  60 **Issue:** The function returns `start + 120`, so the result is only meaningful against the same clock as the input. Nothing says whether that is `time.monotonic()` or `time.time()`. A caller mixing the two gets a silently wrong deadline. Wall-clock time is also vulnerable to NTP steps, which matters when the budget is chosen to fit within a 240 s window. There is no input validation, so NaN, negative or `bool` inputs pass through unchanged. That is acceptable for a helper this small, but the clock contract should be written down.\n  61 **Fix:** Extend the docstring: \"`start_secs` and the return value must come from the same clock; use `time.monotonic()` for deadlines.\"\n  62 \n  63 ### IN-02: Docstring leaves open whether the deadline bounds an in-flight call\n  64 \n  65 **File:** `probe/budget.py:9`\n  66 **Issue:** \"the time at which retries stop\" bounds when new retries stop, not when the last call returns. The comment's rationale is that the probe reports its own failure before the 240 s pager. A call started just before the deadline with no per-call timeout could run past 240 s and defeat that. No caller exists yet, so this is not a defect today. It is a contract gap for the first caller.\n  6