t consent. The gate is open: `01-UAT.md` has `result: [pending]`, `passed: 0, pending: 1`, and VERIFICATION status is `human_needed`. Despite that:\n- `.planning/REQUIREMENTS.md:5` marks BUDGET-01 `[x]` and the traceability row (line 12) says `Complete`.\n- `probe/budget.py:3-4` states the constraints (\"Must cover the upstream's 90-second failover ... stay under the 240-second ... pager\") as settled fact with no note that the value is provisional or agent-chosen.\n- ROADMAP shows the plan as executed, and `.planning/STATE.md:19` (\"Phase complete\") is ahead of the open gate.\n\nShipping this PR makes an agent-made decision look like a human-approved one. The value is not independently verifiable from the repo: the 90s/240s inputs come from the plan text only. The SUMMARY's argument that standing instructions let it resolve the item does not hold for a gate whose purpose is that a human answers. A fact only the operator knows (the real failover and pager times) is exactly what the user's own rules say to ask as a plain factual question, not infer.\n**Fix:** Block merge until a human answers UAT test 1. Then either:\n1. Human confirms the two figures. Record `resolution:` naming who decided, set `result: pass`, and update the comment. Or\n2. Human gives different figures. Edit the constant (and `OFFERED_BUDGETS` only if the offered set changes).\n\nUntil then, make the provisional status visible in code and not only in planning files:\n```python\n# PROVISIONAL (agent-chosen, awaiting human confirmation, see\n# .planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-UAT.md). Rationale, per the plan and\n# not independently verified: must cover a 90 s upstream failover and stay\n# under the 240 s on-call pager escalation.\nDEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS: int = 120\n```\nAlso revert REQUIREMENTS.md BUDGET-01 to unchecked / `In progress` until the gate closes.\n\n## Warnings\n\n### WR-01: Tests cannot detect the constraint the comment claims, or a wrong choice\n\n**File:** `tests/test_budget.py:5-11`\n**Issue:** `test_budget_is_one_of_the_offered_values` accepts 30, 120 and 300. The code comment's constraint (cover 90 s, stay under 240 s) is not tested. VERIFICATION confirms scratch runs with 30 and 300 both pass. So the suite does not test the thing the phase is about, and the decision gate cannot be proven by tests. `test_deadline_adds_the_budget_to_the_start` computes its expected value from the constant (`10.0 + DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS`), so it only checks `+` and would pass for any value or any off-by-constant formulation that uses the same name. I confirmed it is weaker than it looks: it does not pin the budget to a literal. The plan said not to edit this file, so this is a plan-level gap and not an implementation fault.\n**Fix:** Add a test with named bounds that encodes the stated constraint, with its source documented:\n```python\nFAILOVER_SECS, PAGER_SECS = 90, 240  # plan figures, unverified\ndef test_budget_covers_failover_and_precedes_pager(self):\n    self.assertGreater(DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS, FAILOVER_SECS)\n    self.assertLess(DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS, PAGER_SECS)\n```\nand one literal-value check, e.g. `deadline_after(0.0) == 120.0`, if 120 is confirmed.\n\n### WR-02: `deadline_after` accepts values it should not, and does not define its clock\n\n**File:** `probe/budget.py:8-10`\n**Issue:** Verified by running: `deadline_after(float('nan'))` returns `nan`, `deadline_after(True)` returns `121`, and a str raises `TypeError` (acceptable). A `nan` deadline makes any `now < deadline` or `now >= deadline` comparison False, so a caller loops with no bound or never starts, depending on the comparison form. The docstring also does not say whether `start_secs` is `time.monotonic()` or wall-clock (`time.time()`). Mixing them makes the deadline meaningless. It is also silent on whether the deadline bounds an in-flight call. The earlier review recorded this as IN-01/IN-02, and I rate the `nan` case higher because the whole purpose of the function is to bound retries. No caller exists yet in this repo, so impact is latent.\n**Fix:**\n```python\nimport math\ndef deadline_after(start_secs: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Return start_secs + the retry budget. Caller must use one clock\n    (e.g. time.monotonic()) for start and for the comparison.\"\"\"\n    if not math.isfinite(start_secs):\n        raise ValueError(f\"start_secs must be finite, got {start_secs!r}\")\n    return start_secs + DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS\n```\n\n### WR-03: `devflow.toml` base branch contradicts GSD config, and the file is not covered by the plan\n\n**File:** `devflow.toml:1`\n**Issue:** `base_branch = \"integration\"` while `.planning/config.json` has `\"base_branch\": \"main\"`, and the `diff_base` for this review is `main`. Both branches exist on origin. If devflow and GSD tooling each read their own file, PR target and diff base can disagree. `capture_retention = 64` is an unexplained magic number with no comment and no reference in the plan, requirements or summary. This file is in the review scope but in no plan's `files_modified` (the plan lists only `probe/__init__.py` and `probe/budget.py`). I did not confirm what reads `devflow.toml`, so this is a consistency risk and not a proven bug.\n**Fix:** Confirm which branch this PR should target and make both files agree (or document why they differ). Add a comment for `capture_retention` (unit and rationale), or drop it if the scaffold added it without need.\n\n## Info\n\n### IN-01: Planning state is internally inconsistent\n\n**File:** `.planning/STATE.md:3-15` and `.planning/ROADMAP.md`\n**Issue:** STATE has `status: verifying` and `percent: 0` with `completed_phases: 0`, while the body says \"Phase 01 ... EXECUTING\" and \"Phase complete\". `.planning/state.json` is untracked and says \"ready to verify\". These all agree on nothing except that the gate is open. Low impact; flagged so they are not read as a pass signal.\n**Fix:** Let the GSD workflow regenerate them after the UAT gate is answered. Do not hand-edit.\n\n### IN-02: `OFFERED_BUDGETS` and the literal in `budget.py` are two sources of truth\n\n**File:** `tests/test_budget.py:5`\n**Issue:** The offered set (30, 120, 300) is defined in the test, in REQUIREMENTS.md and in the plan options. If the human picks a value outside the set, the test is wrong, not the code. Harmless now.\n**Fix:** Keep as is until the human decision is in. Then replace the membership check with a literal assertion (see WR-01).\n\n---\n\n_Reviewed: 2026-11-02_\n_Reviewer: Claude (gsd-code-reviewer)_\n_Depth: deep_\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1211,"cache_read_input_tokens":54753,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1211,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01HPmTaxDssns4ikT2NoLRFB","agent_id":"a9305b7bd04fe8918","session_id":"uuid-5","uuid":"uuid-499","timestamp":"2026-11-02T11:50:25.480Z","request_id":"req_011Cge3uePhqwyaygBp3U1yP","subagent_type":"gsd-code-reviewer","task_description":"Generalist deep review pass"}
