{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GpSBiRfuST7mF9eLFfqoTX","type":"tool_result","content":"Record cross-file issues with all affected file paths\n</step>\n\n<step name=\"classify_findings\">\nFor each finding, assign severity:\n\n**Critical** — Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, crashes, authentication bypasses:\n- SQL injection, command injection, path traversal\n- Hardcoded secrets in production code\n- Null pointer dereferences that crash\n- Authentication/authorization bypasses\n- Unsafe deserialization\n- Buffer overflows\n\n**Warning** — Logic errors, unhandled edge cases, missing error handling, code smells that could cause bugs:\n- Unchecked array access (`.length` or index without validation)\n- Missing error handling in async/await\n- Off-by-one errors in loops\n- Type coercion issues (`==` vs `===`)\n- Unhandled promise rejections\n- Dead code paths that indicate logic errors\n\n**Info** — Style issues, naming improvements, dead code, unused imports, suggestions:\n- Unused imports/variables\n- Poor naming (single-letter variables except loop counters)\n- Commented-out code\n- TODO/FIXME comments\nreviewed: YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ\ndepth: quick | standard | deep\nfiles_reviewed: N\nfiles_reviewed_list:\n  - path/to/file1.ext\n  - path/to/file2.ext\nfindings:\n  critical: N\n  warning: N\n  info: N\n  total: N\nstatus: clean | issues_found\n---\n```\n\n**3. Body sections (required order):**\n1) `## Structural Findings (fallow)` — only when structural findings were provided; list normalized items first.\n2) `## Narrative Findings (AI reviewer)` — your adversarial findings from direct code review, including any external-reviewer claim you independently verified (`(external: {slug})`, see `load_context` step 5).\n\nNever merge these into one section; structural substrate must stay distinguishable from narrative findings. There is exactly one REVIEW.md schema — an external reviewer lane never gets its own section, and an unverified external claim never appears in REVIEW.md at all.\n\n{\n  \"status\": \"human_needed\",\n  \"next_action\": \"Human verification required. Complete the manual tests in the phase's *-UAT.md, then re-run the verify step until status is passed.\",\n  \"next_command\": \"/gsd-verify-work 01\",\n  \"route\": \"verify-work\"\n}\n## Warnings\n\n### WR-01: REVIEW and VERIFICATION timestamps contradict the git history of their own commits\n\n**File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-REVIEW.md:3,19,71` and `01-VERIFICATION.md:3,24,111`\n**Issue:**\n- `01-REVIEW.md` says `reviewed: 2026-11-02T12:00:00Z`. Its commit `a746e0c` is dated 06:44:22-05:00, which is 11:44:22Z. The claimed review time is about 16 minutes after the file was committed.\n- `01-VERIFICATION.md` says `verified: 2026-11-02T12:10:00Z`. Its commit `8ddef7a` is dated 06:46:37-05:00, which is 11:46:37Z. That is about 23 minutes after the commit.\n- `01-UAT.md` has `started: 11:46:36Z` and `source: [01-VERIFICATION.md]`. It therefore claims to start before the 12:10Z moment its source says it was verified.\n- `01-REVIEW-DISPOSITION.md` (`recorded: 11:44:42Z`) was recorded before the 12:00Z \"review\" it triages.\n- Both timestamps are round values (12:00:00, 12:10:00), which suggests invented rather than measured times.\n- VERIFICATION line 38 says \"Ran it in this session\". That is a verification-time claim anchored to a time that cannot be right.\n\nOrdering by commit time gives: review at about 11:44Z, then disposition, then UAT and VERIFICATION at about 11:46Z.\n**Fix:** Replace the two frontmatter and footer timestamps with the actual generation times, for example `date -u +%FT%TZ` at write time. Alternatively set them to the commit times (`git log -1 --format=%cI a746e0c` and `8ddef7a`). Update the body \"Reviewed:\" and \"Verified:\" lines and the footers to match.\n\n### WR-02: The documents give different bases for who had authority to resolve the blocking-human gate, and the SUMMARY's basis misreads the instruction it cites\n\n**File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-01-SUMMARY.md:89` against `01-UAT.md:23`, `01-VERIFICATION.md:30`, `.planning/STATE.md` (Decisions)\n**Issue:**\n- STATE.md, UAT.md and VERIFICATION.md all say the choice was made \"under DevFlow's resume instruction\" or \"as DevFlow's resume instruction directed\". SUMMARY line 89 says the executor did not treat that relayed message as approval, and \"proceeded because the user's standing global instruction says to answer gate and sign-off items the evidence settles\". The SUMMARY's own line 86 uses the DevFlow-instruction basis, so the SUMMARY disagrees with itself too.\n- The cited global instruction (the \"Reporting\" section of the user's CLAUDE.md) is about a tool's \"needs human\" items: verifier/UAT/gate sign-offs the tool marks for a human by policy. It does not cover a plan's `checkpoint:decision gate=\"blocking-human\"` choice among options. The same file's RULE ZERO (c) says not to pick the option I recommended and proceed without an explicit answer. The SUMMARY therefore leans on a rule that does not apply.\n- SUMMARY line 89 also says the plan's constraints settle the choice \"without any preference involved\". PLAN Task 1 lists a real trade-off (30 s \"frees worker slots quickly\", 120 s \"holds a worker slot for up to two minutes\"). The 30/300 rejections do follow from the plan's 90 s and 240 s figures, so the claim is arguable but overstated.\n- The user's CLAUDE.md is not in the repo, so a reader of the repo cannot check the citation.\n\nThe \"agent decision, not human\" fact is stated consistently everywhere, and that is the safe part. The gap is the stated justification.\n**Fix:** Make SUMMARY line 89 match the other artifacts. Drop the global-instruction citation, or reword it to say no human answered the gate and this was an agent decision under the DevFlow resume instruction. Replace \"without any preference involved\" with \"30 and 300 are excluded by the plan's own figures; the worker-slot cost of 120 was accepted\". Keep the UAT and VERIFICATION wording.\n\n### WR-03: BUDGET-01 is marked Complete in REQUIREMENTS while other artifacts leave its sign-off open\n\n**File:** `.planning/REQUIREMENTS.md:5,11` against `01-VERIFICATION.md:84`, `01-UAT.md:22,29`, `.planning/STATE.md` (Decisions: \"Needs human confirmation\")\n**Issue:**\n- REQUIREMENTS shows `[x] BUDGET-01` and `Complete`.\n- VERIFICATION says \"SATISFIED in code; the choice itself awaits human confirmation\" and `status: human_needed`. UAT is `status: testing` with `passed: 0, pending: 1`.\n- Read literally, the requirement text (\"defines ... chosen from 30, 120 or 300, and deadline_after returns ...\") is met, so the checkbox is defensible. The traceability table is the one place a merge or release gate is likely to read, and it carries no qualifier.\n**Fix:** Either keep `Complete` and add a note to the table row, for example `Complete (value pending human confirmation, see 01-UAT.md)`. Or add a status such as `Complete (unconfirmed)`. Do not leave the bare `Complete`.\n\n### WR-04: `devflow.toml` base branch contradicts `.planning/config.json` and is not mentioned in any phase artifact\n\n**File:** `devflow.toml:1` against `.planning/config.json` (`git.base_branch: \"main\"`)\n**Issue:**\n- `devflow.toml` sets `base_branch = \"integration\"`. `.planning/config.json` says `\"base_branch\": \"main\"` with `\"branching_strategy\": \"none\"`.\n- Local `integration` is exactly ef2d757, the commit that adds `devflow.toml`. `origin/integration` is the same commit. `main` is c7f624a, one commit behind.\n- `git diff main...HEAD` therefore carries `devflow.toml` as a change of this phase, but SUMMARY's \"Files Created/Modified\" and VERIFICATION don't list it. Both are right that it predates the plan (plan_head_before = ef2d757). A PR against `main` would still bring in a config pointing tooling at `integration`.\n- `capture_retention = 64` is undocumented in the phase artifacts.\n- I did not check what DevFlow does with `base_branch`, so I cannot say which file wins.\n**Fix:** Either remove `devflow.toml` from the changes headed to `main`, or record in STATE or SUMMARY that the base branch is `integration` and reconcile `.planning/config.json`. State that `git diff main...HEAD` includes a pre-plan chore commit.\n\n## Info\n\n### IN-01: STATE.md labels the phase two ways and its progress numbers lag its own text\n\n**File:** `.planning/STATE.md:5,10-14,21-23`\n**Issue:**\n- The frontmatter says `status: verifying`. The body says `Phase: 01 ... — EXECUTING`, and the next line says \"Status: Phase complete — ready for verification\".\n- The frontmatter also says `completed_plans: 1` but `completed_phases: 0` and `percent: 0`, while ROADMAP shows the plan `[x]` and \"1/1 plans executed\". `.planning/state.json` says \"0% · ready to verify\". The phase is not verified yet, so 0 percent is internally consistent. The \"EXECUTING\" label is stale.\n**Fix:** Change the body line to `VERIFYING` or `HUMAN-NEEDED`, to match the frontmatter.\n\n### IN-02: STATE \"Performance Metrics\" presents resume-only time as the plan duration\n\n**File:** `.planning/STATE.md:28` against `01-01-SUMMARY.md:49,60`\n**Issue:** STATE records \"Phase 01 P01 | 1 min | 2 tasks | 2 files\". SUMMARY says the 1 minute covers only the resume (Task 2 onward) and that \"Task 1 time in the earlier session was not measured\". The table drops that qualifier. The SUMMARY timestamps give about 36 s from 11:40:49Z to 11:41:25Z.\n**Fix:** Mark the STATE cell as \"1 min (Task 2 only; Task 1 unmeasured)\", or leave it blank.\n\n### IN-03: VALIDATION reports \"Gaps found 0\" and `validated` / `nyquist_compliant: true` while recording an open manual item and known test blind spots\n\n**File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-VALIDATION.md:4-5,53-57,70-78`\n**Issue:**\n- The same file states the suite \"does not distinguish 30, 120 and 300\", and that a hard-coded `10.0` would pass (both re-confirmed above). It has a Manual-Only row that awaits a human. The audit table still says Gaps found 0, Escalated 0.\n- The `created` date was changed from `\"2026-09-28\"` to `\"2026-11-02\"` (main's version has the older date). 2026-11-02 matches the scaffold commit date, so the old value was probably a stale template value, but the change is not mentioned.\n- The \"01-01-02 | ✅ green\" row is accurate.\n**Fix:** Count the manual item and the two known blind spots as partial or escalated gaps, or add a note saying why they are excluded from the count. Mention the `created` change if intentional.\n\n### IN-04: REVIEW cites `budget.py:3-5` for the comment, but the comment is lines 3-4\n\n**File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-REVIEW.md:38,51`\n**Issue:** The source comment occupies lines 3-4. Line 5 is the constant. The range is slightly wider than the comment, but it includes the thing the finding is about, so it is harmless. Every other REVIEW line citation (8-10, 9) checked out.\n**Fix:** None needed. If tidying, use `3-5` consistently as \"comment plus constant\".\n\n## Not verified\n\n## Warnings\n\n### WR-01: `.planning/state.json` is untracked and not ignored, so it will be committed by the next broad `git add`\n\n**File:** `.planning/state.json:1` (untracked); `.gitignore:1-4`\n**Evidence:**\n- `git status --short` shows `?? .planning/state.json`.\n- `git check-ignore -v .planning/state.json` exits 1, so no rule matches.\n- `git add -n -A .` and `git add -n .planning` both print `add '.planning/state.json'`.\n- It is a GSD runtime file (287 bytes, mode 0600, rewritten with an `updated_at` timestamp on each run). Its content now is `contract`, `flavor`, `milestone: null`, `phases: []`, a `next` command hint and `updated_at`. It holds no secrets or paths.\n- The git history for it is empty, so it has never been committed.\n- Other runtime state is handled inconsistently. `.gsd/` is hidden by its own `.gsd/.gitignore` (`*`). `.planning/milestone.lock` is hidden only by `.git/info/exclude` line 7.\n- `.planning/milestone.lock` holds a Claude Code session id and a PID. `.git/info/exclude` is local to this clone and is not shared, so a fresh clone or another machine would not ignore the lock. Nothing in `.gitignore` covers it.\n- `git grep` found neither that session id nor any PID in the 9 branch commits.\n\n**Issue:** Runtime state is kept out of git by local-only mechanisms or by nothing. Content that is harmless today could change in a later GSD version, for example to record a session id. The branch has `commit_docs: true`, so workflow commits stage under `.planning/`.\n**Fix:** Add the runtime files to the tracked `.gitignore`:\n```\n.planning/state.json\n.planning/milestone.lock\n.gsd/\n```\nIf `state.json` is meant to be shared, commit it on purpose and review its content first. Either way it should not be committed by accident. Either way, do not run this fix on this branch without the owner's say-so. It edits a tracked file, and the brief for this review said not to.\n\n## Warnings\n\n### WR-01: The suite cannot tell 30, 120 and 300 apart, so the budget is not guarded at all\n\n**File:** `tests/test_budget.py:5,10-12`\n**Issue:** The only constraint the test places on the constant is \"int and in (30, 120, 300)\". Mutants I ran in a throwaway `git archive` copy (each via `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests`, exit code taken from the command itself, not a pipe tail):\n\n| Mutant | Result |\n|---|---|\n| constant = 30 | exit 0, `Ran 2 tests ... OK` (SURVIVED) |\n| constant = 300 | exit 0, `OK` (SURVIVED) |\n| constant = 45 | exit 1, `FAILED (failures=1)` (killed) |\n| constant = 120.0 | exit 1 (killed, by the `int` check) |\n| constant = True | exit 1 (killed; `True` is not in the tuple, since `True == 1`) |\n| constant = -120, 0 | exit 1 (killed) |\n\nSo the 90-second-failover lower bound and the 240-second pager upper bound in the comment at `probe/budget.py:3-4` are enforced by nothing. A change to 300, which the comment says is wrong, ships green. Negative control: 45 failing shows the suite does run. This is not a defect in the test as the plan's `must_haves` define it (the plan only requires \"one of 30, 120, 300\"), but the comment claims an invariant the check does not hold. The 01-VALIDATION.md approval line already admits this; it is a real gap, not a hidden one.\n**Fix:** Either state the real invariant in the test, for example `self.assertGreaterEqual(DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS, 90)` and `self.assertLess(DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS, 240)`, or soften the comment in `probe/budget.py` so it does not claim an enforced constraint. Because 120 is flagged as an unconfirmed agent choice (01-UAT.md), pinning an exact value in the test is the wrong fix; bounds are.\n\n### WR-02: `deadline_after` has two surviving mutants\n\n**File:** `tests/test_budget.py:12`\n**Issue:** The single sample `deadline_after(10.0)` with `assertEqual` misses:\n\n| Mutant | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `return 10.0 + DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS` (ignores argument) | exit 0, OK (SURVIVED) |\n| `return 130.0` (hard-coded 10+120) | exit 0, OK (SURVIVED) |\n| `int(start_secs) + DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS` (drops fraction) | exit 0, OK (SURVIVED; 10.0 is integral) |\n| `start_secs - BUDGET` | killed |\n| `start_secs * BUDGET` | killed |\n| `start_secs + 300` literal | killed |\n| `return None` | killed |\n| `start_secs + BUDGET*1000` (ms) | killed |\n\nThe `int()` mutant is the most realistic: a deadline that loses sub-second precision on a float clock. The test also computes its expected value from the same constant it tests, so it checks the shape of the formula, not the unit.\n**Fix:** Sample a non-integral start and a second start, for example `for start in (0.0, 10.0, 1234.5): self.assertEqual(deadline_after(start) - start, DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS)` plus `self.assertAlmostEqual(deadline_after(0.25), 0.25 + DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS)`. Confirm each of the three surviving mutants now fails.\n\n### WR-03: `devflow.toml` base_branch conflicts with `.planning/config.json` git.base_branch, and this branch's diff against main carries `devflow.toml` into main\n\n**File:** `devflow.toml:1` vs `.planning/config.json:22`\n**Issue:** `devflow.toml` says `base_branch = \"integration\"`; `.planning/config.json` says `git.base_branch = \"main\"` (with `branching_strategy: \"none\"`). Verified state:\n- `origin/integration` = `ef2d757` (\"chore: configure devflow base branch\", adds only `devflow.toml`). `origin/main` = `c7f624a`, which has no `devflow.toml`.\n- `feature/phase-01` is built on `integration` (`git log main..HEAD` starts with `ef2d757`). `git diff main HEAD --stat -- devflow.toml` shows `devflow.toml | 2 ++` as an addition.\n- `origin/HEAD` is not set (`fatal: ref refs/remotes/origin/HEAD is not a symbolic ref`).\n\nSo a PR or merge to `main` (what GSD's config implies) ships `devflow.toml` into `main`, which then declares `integration` as the base while GSD says `main`. A PR to `integration` (what devflow implies) is coherent. Which tool decides the target is not something I can verify from the repo; I did not run any ship command. The two settings disagree and nothing checks them against each other. Also `capture_retention = 64` is an unexplained magic value with no comment in the file.\n**Fix:** Decide one target and make both files say it. If the target is `integration`, set `.planning/config.json` `git.base_branch` to `\"integration\"`. If it is `main`, drop `devflow.toml` from this branch's PR (rebase onto main, or ship from `integration`). Open the PR with an explicit `--base`.\n\n### WR-04: The \"zero tests ran\" guard is a Python-version accident, and nothing pins the interpreter\n\n**File:** `tests/test_budget.py` (suite as a whole); plan `01-01-PLAN.md` verify command\n**Issue:** With zero discovered tests, `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` exits 5 (`NO TESTS RAN`) on Python 3.15.2 (the `python3` first on PATH here, linuxbrew). I reproduced this three ways in a throwaway tree: empty `tests/`, `tests/budget_test.py` (does not match `test*.py`), and a `test_x.py` whose class does not subclass `TestCase`. All three exited 5, not 0. The exit-5 behavior was added in Python 3.12, so on 3.11 or older the same three cases would exit 0. `/usr/bin/python3` here is 3.14.8; mise and `/bin` shims also exist. No `.python-version`, `pyproject.toml`, or `requires-python` pins anything. The plan's acceptance criterion (\"reports `Ran 2 tests` and `OK`\") would catch it if a human reads the output, but the machine-checkable truth is only the exit code.\nRelated, an all-skipped suite exits 0 (`OK (skipped=1)`) on every version. That is not present today, since the tests have no skip decorators.\n**Fix:** Add a minimal check that asserts the count, such as `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v 2>&1 | grep -q '^Ran [1-9]'` with `pipefail`, or pin the interpreter (`.python-version` or a `requires-python`) to 3.12 or newer. If CI is added later, use `python -W error -m unittest discover -s tests -f` and assert the count.\n\n## Info\n\n### IN-01: Verification commands themselves do not mask failures, but they depend on cwd being the repo root\n\n**File:** `01-01-PLAN.md` verify block, `01-VALIDATION.md` rows\n**Issue:** Every documented verification command is the bare `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v` with no pipe, `tail`, or `|| true`. I confirmed it exits 1 for a failing test (F: import error, `FAILED (errors=1)`), 1 for a nonexistent start dir (`ImportError: Start directory is not importable`), and exits 0 only for real passes. It is only fragile on cwd: run from `/tmp` with an absolute `-s` path it exits 1 with `errors=1`, because `probe` is not on `sys.path`. That is a loud failure, not a false green. The same wrong-direction risk applies only if someone adds `| tail`: `python3 -m unittest discover -s nonexist 2>&1 | tail -1; echo $?` printed `exit(tail)=0` on a failing run. No document does this today. 01-VERIFICATION.md notes that `python3 -I` also fails with `ModuleNotFoundError`, which is the same cwd dependency.\n**Fix:** None needed for this merge. If wrapped in a script, use `set -o pipefail` or read `${PIPESTATUS[0]}`, and add `-t .` so the top-level dir does not depend on cwd.\n\n### IN-02: `.gitignore` leaves `.planning/state.json` untracked and unignored\n\n**File:** `.gitignore:1-4`\n**Issue:** `git status` shows `?? .planning/state.json`, and `git check-ignore -v` returns nothing for it. It is tool-generated (contract, `updated_at` timestamp) and will churn. `.planning/milestone.lock` is excluded only via the local `.git/info/exclude`, which is not shared with clones. Not a CI risk, but a `git add -A` would commit it. This is untracked state that the branch's commits do not include, so it is not part of the diff under review.\n**Fix:** Add `.planning/state.json` and `.planning/milestone.lock` to `.gitignore`, or track them deliberately.\n\n### IN-03: `tests/` has no `__init__.py`, which is fine now but is load-bearing\n\n**File:** `tests/`\n**Issue:** Discovery works because `-s tests` puts `tests` on `sys.path` and `python -m` puts the cwd on it, so `from probe.budget import ...` resolves. It is not a defect today (the suite ran, exit 0, `Ran 2 tests`). It does mean `python3 -m unittest tests.test_budget` or running from another directory behaves differently. Noted only because the phase has no other build config to catch it.\n**Fix:** None required. Document \"run from repo root\" next to the command if more tests are added.\n\n## What the checks do not establish\n\n- I found no CI or packaging, so \"CI would be green\" is untested and unstatable. The suite passing locally (exit 0, `Ran 2 tests`) was re-run by me on Python 3.15.2 and proves only the two assertions above.\n- Mutation coverage is hand-picked (15 mutants), not exhaustive. Surviving mutants are certain; \"killed\" means killed on this interpreter only.\n- I did not verify the 90-second failover or 240-second pager figures; they appear only in the plan, and 01-UAT.md already tracks that as open.\n## Warnings\n\n### WR-01: Requirement and roadmap marked complete while the phase's own verification is `human_needed`\n\n**File:** `.planning/REQUIREMENTS.md:5` and `:12`; `.planning/ROADMAP.md:15-17`; `.planning/STATE.md:5-15`\n**Evidence:**\n- REQUIREMENTS.md: `- [x] **BUDGET-01** ...` and `| BUDGET-01 | Phase 1 | Complete |`.\n- `01-VERIFICATION.md` frontmatter: `status: human_needed`; its coverage row for BUDGET-01 reads \"SATISFIED in code; the choice itself awaits human confirmation\".\n- `01-UAT.md`: `status: testing`, `passed: 0`, `pending: 1`.\n- STATE.md is internally inconsistent: `status: verifying`, `completed_phases: 0`, `percent: 0`, but body says `Phase 01 ... — EXECUTING` and `Status: Phase complete — ready for verification`.\n\n**Issue:** A reader of REQUIREMENTS/ROADMAP alone would conclude BUDGET-01 is done. The requirement text itself says the value is \"chosen from 30, 120 or 300\", and that choice is the unconfirmed item. The `[x]` and \"Complete\" were written by the execute step before verification ran, and nothing was walked back after verification returned `human_needed`. STATE.md simultaneously says EXECUTING, complete, verifying and 0%. Anyone deciding to merge on the strength of \"Complete\" would be relying on a status the record does not support.\n**Fix:** Either leave BUDGET-01 as `Pending` / `[ ]` until UAT test 1 is resolved by a human, or add a visible qualifier (\"Complete in code; value awaiting human confirmation, see 01-UAT.md\"). Make STATE.md body match its frontmatter (one status, and a percent that agrees with `completed_plans: 1`).\n\n### WR-02: SUMMARY says \"Deviations from Plan: None\" while recording that a `blocking-human` gate was answered by an agent\n\n**File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-01-SUMMARY.md:77-79` versus `:61-67`; plan `01-01-PLAN.md:10,29`\n**Evidence:** Plan has `autonomous: false` and Task 1 `gate=\"blocking-human\"`. SUMMARY \"Decisions Made\" says \"no human answered it\" and that the agent chose; SUMMARY \"Deviations\" says \"None - plan executed exactly as written.\"\n**Issue:** The two sections contradict each other. Resolving a blocking-human gate without a human is a departure from the plan's own control, and the deviation section is where a downstream reader (or a merge reviewer skimming) looks for it. The SUMMARY also grounds the authority in \"the user's standing global instruction\", and the VERIFICATION and UAT cite \"DevFlow's resume instruction\" as the trigger. Neither is a recorded approval of this decision by a person, and neither can be checked from the repo. The artifacts do not hide this, but the \"None\" line understates it.\n**Fix:** Replace \"None\" with an explicit deviation: \"Task 1 (`blocking-human`) was resolved by the orchestrator agent, not a human; see Decisions Made and 01-UAT.md.\" Keep the existing disclosure of the authority basis.\n\n### WR-03: Review and verification timestamps are later than the commits that contain them\n\n**File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-REVIEW.md:3`; `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-VERIFICATION.md:4`\n**Evidence:**\n```\n01-REVIEW.md       reviewed: 2026-11-02T12:00:00Z   committed a746e0c 2026-11-02T06:44:22-05:00 (= 11:44:22Z)\n01-VERIFICATION.md verified:  2026-11-02T12:10:00Z   committed 8ddef7a 2026-11-02T06:46:37-05:00 (= 11:46:37Z)\n```\nThe disposition file records `recorded: 2026-11-02T11:44:42.773Z`, which is earlier than the review's stated `reviewed` time of 12:00:00Z. The round values (12:00:00, 12:10:00) look typed rather than measured.\n**Issue:** Each artifact states a time that had not yet happened when it was committed. These are provenance fields that downstream tooling and humans use to order events (review before disposition before verification). They are not material to the merge decision by themselves, but they are