 constant to 30 or 300, the two values the plan rejects for violating exactly this invariant, still leaves the suite green, so a one-character change would silently break the stated contract. The summary notes this gap itself (D2: \"tests only check that the value is one of 30/120/300\"). Test files are out of scope for this review except where they affect reliability, and this one does.","-**Fix:** Name the two bounds as constants and assert them, either at import or in the test:","+**File:** `probe/budget.py:3-5`, `tests/test_budget.py:5,10-12`","+**Issue:** The comment says the budget must cover the 90-second failover and stay under the 240-second pager. The tests only assert membership in `(30, 120, 300)`. In a throwaway copy, constants of 30 and 300 both exit 0 with `OK`, while 45, 120.0, True, -120 and 0 fail. A change to 300, which the comment says is wrong, would ship green. The plan's must-haves only ask for membership, so this is a gap in the plan rather than an executor fault.","+**Fix:** Assert the bounds in a test, with their source noted, and do not pin 120 until a human confirms it:"," ```python","-UPSTREAM_FAILOVER_SECS: int = 90","-PAGER_ESCALATION_SECS: int = 240","-DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS: int = 120","-assert UPSTREAM_FAILOVER_SECS <= DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS < PAGER_ESCALATION_SECS","+FAILOVER_SECS, PAGER_SECS = 90, 240  # plan figures, unverified","+self.assertGreaterEqual(DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS, FAILOVER_SECS)","+self.assertLess(DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS, PAGER_SECS)"," ```","-Prefer putting the bounds assertion in `tests/test_budget.py` over a module-level `assert`, which `python -O` strips.","+The plan forbids editing `tests/test_budget.py`, so this needs a follow-up plan or a new test file."," "," ### WR-02: Unverified, agent-chosen value documented as settled"," ","-**File:** `probe/budget.py:3-5`","-**Issue:** The source comment presents the 90 s and 240 s figures as established facts. The SUMMARY records that these came only from the plan's `<context>` and were not checked against any other source. It also records that Task 1 (`gate=\"blocking-human\"`) was resolved by the orchestrator agent and not by a human. A later maintainer reading only the code would take the number as vetted. The value is reversible, but until a human confirms it, the code gives no hint that it is provisional.","-**Fix:** Do not ship this as final until a human confirms or changes the value. Until then, make the status visible in the comment, for example \"Provisional: 90 s/240 s figures from plan 01-01, unverified; pending human confirmation.\" Remove the note once confirmed.","+**File:** `probe/budget.py:3-5`; gate at `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-01-PLAN.md:10,29`","+**Issue:** Plan Task 1 is `gate=\"blocking-human\"` with `autonomous: false`. No human answered it, and an agent chose 120. `01-UAT.md` shows `result: [pending]`, with 0 passed and 1 pending. The planning documents say this plainly, but the source comment does not: it states both figures as fact and gives no sign that the value is provisional, and it is the only record a later maintainer is likely to read. The reasoning holds on the plan's figures, since 30 is below 90, 300 is above 240, and only 120 fits. The figures themselves are unchecked.","+**Fix:** A human who knows the real upstream and pager must answer UAT test 1 before merge. Until then, mark the comment provisional:","+```python","+# PROVISIONAL: agent-chosen, awaiting human confirmation (see 01-UAT.md).","+# Plan figures, not independently verified: must cover a 90 s upstream failover","+# and stay under the 240 s on-call pager escalation.","+```"," ","+### WR-03: BUDGET-01 is marked Complete while its sign-off is open","+","+**File:** `.planning/REQUIREMENTS.md:5,12`, against `01-VERIFICATION.md:84` and `01-UAT.md:22,29`","+**Issue:** REQUIREMENTS shows `[x]` and `Complete` with no qualifier. VERIFICATION says \"SATISFIED in code; the choice itself awaits human confirmation\". The requirement's literal text is met, so the checkbox is defensible, but the traceability row is where someone deciding on a merge would look, and it omits the open item.","+**Fix:** Qualify the row, for example `Complete in code; value pending human confirmation (01-UAT.md)`, or leave it pending until UAT test 1 is answered.","+","+### WR-04: SUMMARY reports no deviations and gives an inconsistent basis for the agent's authority","+","+**File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-01-SUMMARY.md:86,89,94`, against `01-UAT.md:23` and `01-VERIFICATION.md:30`","+**Issue:** Line 94 says \"None - plan executed exactly as written\", while line 86 says an agent answered a `blocking-human` gate. Line 89 bases the decision on a standing instruction about tool-marked sign-offs, which does not obviously cover a plan's choice among options. UAT and VERIFICATION base it on DevFlow's resume instruction instead. The repository can check neither basis. Line 89's \"without any preference involved\" also overstates the case, because the plan lists a real worker-slot cost for 120.","+**Fix:** Record the agent's resolution of Task 1 as a deviation, give one basis across all documents, and replace the \"preference\" wording with \"30 and 300 are excluded by the plan's figures; the worker-slot cost of 120 was accepted\".","+","+### WR-05: Review and verification timestamps are later than the commits that contain them","+","+**File:** `01-VERIFICATION.md:3,24,111`; the earlier version of this report","+**Issue:** VERIFICATION says `verified: 2026-11-02T12:10:00Z`, but its commit `8ddef7a` is dated 11:46:37Z, about 23 minutes earlier. `01-UAT.md` names VERIFICATION as its source yet says it started at 11:46:36Z. The earlier review had the same fault (`12:00:00Z` in a commit dated 11:44:22Z), and this report replaces it. The round values look typed rather than measured. They do not affect the merge decision, but other tools and readers use these fields to order events.","+**Fix:** Write these fields from `date -u +%FT%TZ` when the file is generated, or derive them from the commit.","+","+### WR-06: `devflow.toml` and `.planning/config.json` name different base branches, and a PR to main adds the file","+","+**File:** `devflow.toml:1`, `.planning/config.json`","+**Issue:** `devflow.toml` says `base_branch = \"integration\"`; `.planning/config.json` says `\"base_branch\": \"main\"`. This branch is built on `integration` (`ef2d757`), and `main` has no `devflow.toml`, so a PR to `main` adds a file that points tooling at a different branch than GSD uses. No phase document mentions the file. `capture_retention = 64` is also unexplained.","+**Fix:** Decide what `main` should contain, then either make the two settings agree or record why they differ. Pass an explicit `--base` when opening the PR.","+","+### WR-07: The deadline test leaves realistic mutants alive","+","+**File:** `tests/test_budget.py:12`","+**Issue:** The single sample `deadline_after(10.0)` does not catch `int(start_secs) + DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS`, `10.0 + DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS` or a hard-coded `130.0`. All three exit 0. The `int()` mutant is the most realistic, because it silently drops sub-second precision. Twelve of the 15 mutants tried were killed.","+**Fix:** In a new test file, sample several non-integral starts, then confirm the three mutants fail:","+```python","+for start in (0.0, 0.25, 1234.5):","+    self.assertEqual(deadline_after(start) - start, DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS)","+```","+","+### WR-08: A NaN start time removes the retry bound","+","+**File:** `probe/budget.py:8-10`","+**Issue:** `deadline_after(float('nan'))` returns `nan`, and both `nan < x` and `nan >= x` are False. A loop that stops on `now >= deadline` would therefore never stop. `deadline_after(True)` returns 121. No caller exists yet, so the impact is latent. A clock source does not normally return NaN, so this matters only if the value comes from external data.","+**Fix:** Reject non-finite input with `math.isfinite`, or state the requirement in the docstring when the first caller is written.","+","+### WR-09: Runtime state is not reliably kept out of git","+","+**File:** `.gitignore:1-4`, `.planning/state.json`, `.planning/milestone.lock`","+**Issue:** `.planning/state.json` is untracked and matches no ignore rule, and `git add -n .planning` would stage it. Its content is harmless today: a command hint and a timestamp. `.planning/milestone.lock` holds a Claude Code session id and a PID, and is hidden only by `.git/info/exclude`, which a fresh clone does not have. Neither file is in any branch commit.","+**Fix:** Add `.planning/state.json` and `.planning/milestone.lock` to the tracked `.gitignore`, or track `state.json` on purpose after reviewing what it holds.","+","+### WR-10: Zero discovered tests only fail on Python 3.12 or newer, and no interpreter is pinned","+","+**File:** repository as a whole; verify command at `01-01-PLAN.md:67`","+**Issue:** With an empty `tests/`, a misnamed file or a class that is not a `TestCase`, the command exited 5 on Python 3.15.2. That exit status was added in 3.12, so older interpreters would exit 0, and nothing pins the version. An all-skipped suite exits 0 on every version, though no test skips today. The repository has no CI, so these runs are manual.","+**Fix:** Pin the interpreter (for example `.python-version`) to 3.12 or newer, and assert the test count if the command is ever scripted.","+"," ## Info"," "," ### IN-01: `deadline_after` does not say which clock `start_secs` is on"," "," **File:** `probe/budget.py:8-10`","-**Issue:** The function returns `start + 120`, so the result is only meaningful against the same clock as the input. Nothing says whether that is `time.monotonic()` or `time.time()`. A caller mixing the two gets a silently wrong deadline. Wall-clock time is also vulnerable to NTP steps, which matters when the budget is chosen to fit within a 240 s window. There is no input validation, so NaN, negative or `bool` inputs pass through unchanged. That is acceptable for a helper this small, but the clock contract should be written down.","-**Fix:** Extend the docstring: \"`start_secs` and the return value must come from the same clock; use `time.monotonic()` for deadlines.\"","+**Issue:** The result is only meaningful against the same clock as the input. Mixing `time.monotonic()` and `time.time()` gives a silently wrong deadline, and wall-clock time can step.","+**Fix:** State in the docstring that both values must come from one clock, and recommend `time.monotonic()`."," "," ### IN-02: Docstring leaves open whether the deadline bounds an in-flight call"," "," **File:** `probe/budget.py:9`","-**Issue:** \"the time at which retries stop\" bounds when new retries stop, not when the last call returns. The comment's rationale is that the probe reports its own failure before the 240 s pager. A call started just before the deadline with no per-call timeout could run past 240 s and defeat that. No caller exists yet, so this is not a defect today. It is a contract gap for the first caller.","-**Fix:** State in the docstring whether callers must cap each attempt at the remaining budget (`deadline - now`), or add a per-attempt timeout when the retry loop is written.","+**Issue:** A call started just before the deadline with no per-call timeout could run past 240 seconds, which would defeat the comment's stated purpose. No caller exists yet.","+**Fix:** State that callers must cap each attempt at the remaining budget, or add a per-attempt timeout when the retry loop is written."," ","+### IN-03: STATE.md labels the phase several ways","+","+**File:** `.planning/STATE.md:5,14,23`","+**Issue:** The frontmatter says `status: verifying` and `percent: 0`, the body says `EXECUTING`, and the next line says \"Phase complete — ready for verification\". None of these should be read as a pass.","+**Fix:** Let GSD regenerate the file once the UAT item is answered.","+","+### IN-04: VALIDATION's wording can be read as a human sign-off","+","+**File:** `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-VALIDATION.md`","+**Issue:** It shows `status: validated`, `nyquist_compliant: true`, `Gaps found 0` and an \"Approval\" line, alongside an open manual item and known test blind spots. The approval is labelled as an automated audit, so this is not a false claim. The `created` date also changed from 2026-09-28 to 2026-11-02 without comment; the new date matches the git history.","+**Fix:** Word it as \"automated audit only; no human sign-off. Open: UAT test 1.\"","+","+### IN-05: STATE reports resume-only time as the plan's duration","+","+**File:** `.planning/STATE.md`, against `01-01-SUMMARY.md:49,60`","+**Issue:** STATE records \"1 min\", but SUMMARY says that covers only the resume and that Task 1's time was not measured.","+**Fix:** Add the qualifier or leave the cell blank.","+","+### IN-06: No CI or repository-level secret scanning","+","+**File:** repository as a whole","+**Issue:** The repository has no CI workflow, tracked pre-commit hook or dependency manifest. Tests and secret scans run only when someone runs them by hand.","+**Fix:** If this repository outlives the probe, add a tracked `gitleaks` pre-commit hook and a workflow that runs the test suite.","+","+### IN-07: Tests depend on the working directory and repeat the offered values","+","+**File:** `tests/test_budget.py:5`, `tests/`","+**Issue:** `tests/` has no `__init__.py`, and imports resolve only when run from the repository root; running elsewhere fails visibly rather than passing. The offered values also appear in the test, REQUIREMENTS and the plan, so a human choosing another value would have to update all three.","+**Fix:** None needed now. Document \"run from the repository root\" if more tests are added.","+"," ---"," ","-_Reviewed: 2026-11-02T12:00:00Z_","-_Reviewer: Claude (gsd-code-reviewer)_","-_Depth: standard_","+_Reviewed: 2026-11-02T11:52:35Z_","+_Reviewer: Claude (gsd-code-reviewer, five finder passes merged by the orchestrator)_","+_Depth: deep_"]}],"originalFile":"---\nphase: 01-retry-budget\nreviewed: 2026-11-02T12:00:00Z\ndepth: standard\nfiles_reviewed: 2\nfiles_reviewed_list:\n  - probe/__init__.py\n  - probe/budget.py\nfindings:\n  critical: 0\n  warning: 2\n  info: 2\n  total: 4\nstatus: issues_found\n---\n\n# Phase 1: Code Review Report\n\n**Reviewed:** 2026-11-02T12:00:00Z\n**Depth:** standard\n**Files Reviewed:** 2\n**Status:** issues_found\n\n## Summary\n\nReviewed `probe/__init__.py` (empty, 0 bytes, as the plan specifies) and `probe/budget.py` (a typed constant plus a one-line `deadline_after` helper). The code does what the plan says. `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v` was re-run in this review: exit 0, `Ran 2 tests`, `OK`. There are no security surfaces (no I/O, no parsing, no secrets) and no logic defects in the arithmetic.\n\nThe concerns are about what the code asserts and what guards it:\n- The 120 s value is the result of a plan decision gated `blocking-human` that no human answered (per 01-01-SUMMARY.md). The source comment states the justifying figures as fact.\n- Nothing enforces the invariant the comment describes.\n\nThe test run shows only that the code matches the tests. It does not show that 120 s is correct for the real upstream. A scratch run with `python3 -I` failed with `ModuleNotFoundError` because `-I` drops the current directory from `sys.path`. That is an artifact of my isolation flag, not a defect, and the plan's own command passes.\n\n## Warnings\n\n### WR-01: Constraint stated as fact in code is unenforced; tests accept 30 and 300\n\n**File:** `probe/budget.py:3-5`\n**Issue:** The comment says the budget \"must cover the upstream's 90-second failover and stay under the 240-second pager escalation\". That is a hard invariant, but it exists only as prose. `tests/test_budget.py` asserts only membership in `(30, 120, 300)`. Editing the constant to 30 or 300, the two values the plan rejects for violating exactly this invariant, still leaves the suite green, so a one-character change would silently break the stated contract. The summary notes this gap itself (D2: \"tests only check that the value is one of 30/120/300\"). Test files are out of scope for this review except where they affect reliability, and this one does.\n**Fix:** Name the two bounds as constants and assert them, either at import or in the test:\n```python\nUPSTREAM_FAILOVER_SECS: int = 90\nPAGER_ESCALATION_SECS: int = 240\nDEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS: int = 120\nassert UPSTREAM_FAILOVER_SECS <= DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS < PAGER_ESCALATION_SECS\n```\nPrefer putting the bounds assertion in `tests/test_budget.py` over a module-level `assert`, which `python -O` strips.\n\n### WR-02: Unverified, agent-chosen value documented as settled\n\n**File:** `probe/budget.py:3-5`\n**Issue:** The source comment presents the 90 s and 240 s figures as established facts. The SUMMARY records that these came only from the plan's `<context>` and were not checked against any other source. It also records that Task 1 (`gate=\"blocking-human\"`) was resolved by the orchestrator agent and not by a human. A later maintainer reading only the code would take the number as vetted. The value is reversible, but until a human confirms it, the code gives no hint that it is provisional.\n**Fix:** Do not ship this as final until a human confirms or changes the value. Until then, make the status visible in the comment, for example \"Provisional: 90 s/240 s figures from plan 01-01, unverified; pending human confirmation.\" Remove the note once confirmed.\n\n## Info\n\n### IN-01: `deadline_after` does not say which clock `start_secs` is on\n\n**File:** `probe/budget.py:8-10`\n**Issue:** The function returns `start + 120`, so the result is only meaningful against the same clock as the input. Nothing says whether that is `time.monotonic()` or `time.time()`. A caller mixing the two gets a silently wrong deadline. Wall-clock time is also vulnerable to NTP steps, which matters when the budget is chosen to fit within a 240 s window. There is no input validation, so NaN, negative or `bool` inputs pass through unchanged. That is acceptable for a helper this small, but the clock contract should be written down.\n**Fix:** Extend the docstring: \"`start_secs` and the return value must come from the same clock; use `time.monotonic()` for deadlines.\"\n\n### IN-02: Docstring leaves open whether the deadline bounds an in-flight call\n\n**File:** `probe/budget.py:9`\n**Issue:** \"the time at which retries stop\" bounds when new retries stop, not when the last call returns. The comment's rationale is that the probe reports its own failure before the 240 s pager. A call started just before the deadline with no per-call timeout could run past 240 s and defeat that. No caller exists yet, so this is not a defect today. It is a contract gap for the first caller.\n**Fix:** State in the docstring whether callers must cap each attempt at the remaining budget (`deadline - now`), or add a per-attempt timeout when the retry loop is written.\n\n---\n\n_Reviewed: 2026-11-02T12:00:00Z_\n_Reviewer: Claude (gsd-code-reviewer)_\n_Depth: standard_\n","userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RTDDxHmqXTVbkVqtkjj9Gy","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
